{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788195561,"nonce":"tpkisJEO/nbisk/dpHkVYCACdss=","title":"`world-fixture {steps:[\"bench\",\"bill\"]}` reads as a pipeline and is not one. The","message":"`bill` step does not receive the bench the `bench` step just spawned — it calls\n`WorldFixtureVerbs.FindBench`, which with no `bench` arg returns the FIRST\n`TableButcher` in `map.listerThings.ThingsInGroup(PotentialBillGiver)`.\n\nOn a virgin map those are the same table and everything looks correct. On the\nSECOND such call in a session they are not.\n\nCONFIRMED LIVE on BORGES, 2026-08-31, while running 32b9e01's acceptance. Raw\nenvelope from one `world-fixture {steps:[\"bench\",\"bill\"],skill_min:0,target_count:200}`:\n\n    \"bench\": { \"id\": 23492, \"at\": [119,115], \"expect_bills\": 0 },\n    \"bill\":  { \"bench_id\": 23491, \"expect_bills\": 4 }\n\nThe `bench` block reports the table it made (#23492). The `bill` block reports\nthat it put the bills on #23491 — the bench left over from the previous call —\nand that that stack now holds FOUR bills, two from each run. #23492 has none.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND UNTIDINESS. The caller reads `data.bench.id` to know what\nto act on, because that is the only id the `bench` step publishes. So the caller\ntargets an EMPTY bench. In 32b9e01's acceptance that made `orders` return\n`total:0`, which is indistinguishable from the defect under test and passes its\nM1 check vacuously. A fixture that silently stages the wrong object turns a\ngreen run into no evidence at all — `2f2796e`'s concern, in the fixture layer.\n\nThe verb's own error message already asserts the chaining it does not do:\n`\"bill needs a bill giver (run the `bench` step first)\"`.\n\nRelated: the `bench` step publishes `expect_bills: 0` unconditionally, even in a\ncall whose very next step adds two bills to it. If the steps chained, that field\nwould be wrong in the common case.\n\nWORKAROUND IN USE, so nothing is blocked: two calls, the second naming the\nbench, plus an assert that `data.bill.bench_id` came back equal to\n`data.bench.id`. See `accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1` `New-FixtureBench`.\n\nAcceptance\n\n- Within ONE `world-fixture` call, a later step operates on the object an\n  earlier step in that same call created. Concretely: `{steps:[\"bench\",\"bill\"]}`\n  run TWICE on one map leaves two benches with two bills each, never one bench\n  with four.\n- `data.bill.bench_id` equals `data.bench.id` whenever both steps run in the\n  same call, and there is a test that would fail if it did not.\n- An explicit `bench` arg still wins over the chained value, so the two-call\n  form keeps working.\n- `data.bench.expect_bills` reflects the bills present when the call RETURNS,\n  not 0 unconditionally — or the field is removed in favour of the `bill`\n  block's own count, with the choice recorded.\n- Audit the other steps for the same shape. `stockpiles`, `growing`, `research`\n  and `letter` all resolve their targets independently; say in the closing\n  comment which of them can and cannot be chained, rather than fixing `bill`\n  alone and leaving the class open.","files":null}]}